WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:06.289 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the brain is a magical organ for us to  hear sound it does things complex and  incredible but not unknowable the brain  takes sound waves which journey through  the air and through our skulls and  transforms them into sound in our minds  this sonic Odyssey is beautiful filled  with weird landscapes unexpected  encounters catastrophe and joyful  destinations all like a strange aural  fairytale hearing evolved because it  helped animals to survive hearing  allowed predators to find prey and eat  them and hearing allowed prey to spy on  predators in to escape the floppy piece  of skin attached to the size of our head  is our outer ear or Pina it collects  sounds from a wide area of our  environment like a reflector disk of a  satellite dish in humans the pinna is in  a fixed position but in many animals the  pinna are highly flexible and can be  oriented toward a source of sound  [Music]  curiously our auditory system has  evolved beyond its strict utility of  survival for example music through its  perception as sound by our auditory  system evokes sensations and emotions  sound is produced when something  vibrates causing variations in air  pressure particles of air collide with  other particles and the wave of movement  bouncing from particle to particle  passes outward through the air this is  called a sound wave and now our brave  sound waves are approaching the outer  ear and are truly starting their journey  once they've entered the ear through the  pinna they passed down the air-filled  auditory canal before hitting the  eardrum as the sound waves strike the  eardrum the vibrations in the air are  transferred through that membrane into a  series of little bones called the  ossicles there are three bones in this  group the malleus incus and stapes also  known as the hammer anvil and stirrup  they function to transmit sounds from  the air-filled outer ear into the  fluid-filled inner ear by allowing the  sound to enter them through the eardrum  vibrates rhythm and then exit into the  inner ear via a second membrane called  the oval window the inner ear contains  the sensory organs for both hearing as  well as for our sense of balance the  cochlea an important organ for the  auditory process is snail shaped and if  rolled up is only about the size of a  pea  inside the cochlea is the organ of Corti  which sits on the basilar membrane and  below the tectorial membrane and inside  the organ of Corti are thousands of  auditory hair cells these hair cells are  in fact receptor neurons called  stereocilia these hair cells convert the  motion energy of sound waves into  electrical signals that are communicated  to the auditory nerve from there the  signal goes into structures deeper in  the brain until at last it reaches the  auditory cortex where we consciously  experience sound to back up when our  sound waves enter the fluid-filled inner  ear they are thrust into a storm a storm  of vibrations that they are in fact  creating the basilar membrane below them  is violently shaking and tossing them  the tectorial membrane above them is  moving back and forth pushing and  threatening  and in the midst of this maelstrom as  they are broken apart they spiral  through the inner ear and the sound  waves finally begin to weaken the inner  ear absorbs all their kinetic strength  and the sound waves begin to realize  that this will be their end now  finally in the chaos of the inner ear  they disappear  the sound waves have ceased to be but  though the vibrations are now no more  they are not completely gone  they have metamorphosed into something  new into neural activity and they come  to life again in the cortex of the brain  in the consciousness of our mugs their  journey is over the brain is taken what  was outside and made it inside  [Music]

